1
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:10,560
What's up everyone! We are going to look at a second very important topic in economics. This is supply or when firms have the willingness and ability to sell a product.

2
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:12,080
Let's jump into it.

3
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:21,960
We have a supply schedule on the left and we are selling pizzas. You'll notice we have two variables, price and quantity supplied.

4
00:00:22,520 --> 00:00:33,120
Also at the price decreases from $25 all the way down to $5 the quantity supplied also decreases from 40,000 to 0.

5
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:44,720
This is what we call the law of supply, that is there is an direct relationship between the price of a good and the quantity supplied of that good, ceteris paribus.

6
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:50,840
This is an important concept to firms as it shows there is a price that is too low for them to sell.

7
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:57,480
Of course they can't charge the maximum price as they may not get that many buyers.

8
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:02,800
Let's go ahead and sketch in these numbers in our graph to the right.

9
00:01:03,640 --> 00:01:07,360
We're going to first label vertical axis was price.

10
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:11,200
And the horizontal axis with quantity supplied.

11
00:01:13,080 --> 00:01:14,680
Now let's put in the numbers.

12
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:31,520
And let's put in some dots.

13
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:38,800
And we'll connect those dots.

14
00:01:39,760 --> 00:01:46,680
And there you have a supply curve. This curve shows the pairing of the individual prices and their respective quantities.

15
00:01:47,840 --> 00:01:52,080
As we move down along the supply curve you'll notice

16
00:01:52,720 --> 00:01:57,080
price falls, and the quantity supplied also falls.

17
00:01:57,600 --> 00:02:10,600
As we move up along the supply curve the price rises as does the quantity supplied. This again shows the willingness of those producers to sell more at those higher prices.

18
00:02:11,120 --> 00:02:13,920
Basically any time the price of a good changes

19
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:22,600
we get a change in quantity supplied so to change the quantity supplied, this is important, we have to have a change

20
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:23,880
price.

21
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:26,960
But what happens if something other than

22
00:02:28,120 --> 00:02:41,360
price of the good changes? Suppose more firms enter the market. This causes a complete change in the supply curve. The same can be said if firms leave as well. In each case the supply curve will shift to the right

23
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:43,640
as an increase in supply.

24
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:47,040
Or, it will shift to the left

25
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:49,240
as a decrease in

26
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:50,680
supply.

27
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:58,640
In this next table we have a new quantity, one that changed because of some factor other than price change.

28
00:02:59,160 --> 00:03:06,720
If firms enter the market then at price more firms are willing to sell more pizzas. This shift the supply curve to the left,

29
00:03:07,240 --> 00:03:08,040
to the right.

30
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:19,480
However if firms left the market than the supply curve shifts to the left as there are not enough firms to sell the same amount of pizza as before at the same price. Let's relabel our graph. P for

31
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:20,560
price.

32
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:22,120
Q for quantity.

33
00:03:23,240 --> 00:03:29,120
Our supply curve is upward sloping and we will have an increase in our supply curve,

34
00:03:30,400 --> 00:03:32,480
so we will shift it to the right.

35
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,080
And a decrease in our supply curve so we will shift it to the left.

36
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:45,280
There are quite a few determinants of supply. We've already mentioned one and that is a change in the number firms in the market.

37
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:50,960
We saw that an increase in firms leads to an increase in supply.

38
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:56,720
And a decrease in firms leads to a decrease in supply.

39
00:03:57,560 --> 00:04:00,520
Our next determine is a change in technology.

40
00:04:01,200 --> 00:04:07,320
where technology is bringing the factors of production, like capital and labor, together to make production more efficient.

41
00:04:07,880 --> 00:04:12,800
Suppose a new technological process is introduced to make pizza faster.

42
00:04:13,760 --> 00:04:15,200
This will increase the supply curve.

43
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:22,000
If we were first that technology then we would have a decrease in the supply curve.

44
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:27,600
What happens if the price of labor, that is wages and salaries, rise?

45
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:30,840
This increases the cost of production for a firm.

46
00:04:31,440 --> 00:04:35,400
Therefore they see a rising cost but maybe no rise in revenue.

47
00:04:36,040 --> 00:04:44,360
Firms then don't want to produce as much so we can see any increase in input prices causes a decrease in supply.

48
00:04:44,960 --> 00:04:46,000
The opposite is true.

49
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:54,040
If labor costs were to fall then we would see the supply curve increase. Let's take a here just to clean this graph up.

50
00:04:59,200 --> 00:04:59,640
Okay.

51
00:05:00,160 --> 00:05:09,120
People like to plan for the future and so do firms. Firms like to know how the price of their product will change. They want to know if it will increase or if it will decrease.

52
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:17,440
If our p;izza makers expect prices to rise in the future

53
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:22,480
they'll be less likely to sell more pizzas today. so the supply will decrease.

54
00:05:23,040 --> 00:05:31,720
However is our pizza makers expect prices in the future to fall then they're more likely to sell pizzas today to capture that price.

55
00:05:32,840 --> 00:05:36,520
So we will see am increase in the supply curve or a shift to the right.

56
00:05:37,200 --> 00:05:40,720
Our last determinant deals with changes in taxes and subsidies.

57
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:51,720
Firms often have taxes levied on their products from state and local governments. If a firm has to pay the tax then it has an added cost.

58
00:05:53,080 --> 00:06:01,440
Now, they don't want to produce as much because of this higher cost of production. So the supply curve will decrease as a result.

59
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:08,040
If those governments remove those taxes then firm costs decrease and the supply curve

60
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:09,400
increases.

61
00:06:10,480 --> 00:06:15,680
Subsidies are like a benefit to production in that they lower the cost of production.

62
00:06:16,760 --> 00:06:21,280
If the government awards firms a subsidy to our pizza makers

63
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:25,600
then they are able to produce more, thus increasing the supply curve.

64
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:32,600
However if the government takes the pizza subsidy away from our firms then our supply curve

65
00:06:33,760 --> 00:06:34,040
decreases.

66
00:06:34,720 --> 00:06:40,400
You should have noticed that each of these determines the price of pizza never changed it was some other factor--the determinants--

67
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:43,400
that cause a change in the supply for

68
00:06:43,920 --> 00:06:44,240
pizza.

69
00:06:44,760 --> 00:07:15,080
This is important to note the distinction between a change in the quantity supplied, which is a movement along the supply curve, and is caused by a change in the price of a good versus a change in supply which is a shift of the entire supply curve either as an increase which is a shift to the right or a decrease which is a shift to the left. And this is caused by those determinants of supply.

70
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:18,640
Alright that's it. We'll see you in the next video.